A window inside the frame Qt draws around it (client-side decorations, where
the desktop draws no frame) that an engine draws as a pair is shown as a pair
(ORDER 583). Qt Quick asks for the two views of such a window on a format that
did not ask for stereo, and the frame's blit took its number of eyes and its
OpenGL API from that format, so it drew the left eye alone from an OpenGL ES
context and the EGL implementation presented one view. The blit now follows the
window: a window with a slot per eye is blitted eye by eye from a desktop
OpenGL context, made the first time it is needed, and the slots follow the
surface's views when the surface is made again (o583-0001). Every other window
is blitted as before.

Where no desktop OpenGL context can be made for that frame, the window is shown
flat and the person is told through the problems engine, once for the driver
(key one-view:opengl-frame:DRIVER), in the ten languages; the problem ends when
a window inside Qt's frame gets its frame into both views from that driver
(o583-0002).
